1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,694
December, nineteen twenty-one.

2
00:00:02,694 --> 00:00:12,798
On the edge of Tunis, a local official named Paul Gielly notices a man on his hands and knees in the moonlight, prising carved stones out of the ground.

3
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:20,145
What lay beneath those stones would become one of the most argued-over sites in archaeology:

4
00:00:20,145 --> 00:00:22,051
urn after urn of burnt bones.

5
00:00:22,051 --> 00:00:24,679
The bones of babies, and of young lambs.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,846
Two teams of scientists have since studied the same remains.

7
00:00:29,846 --> 00:00:32,218
They reached conflicting conclusions.

8
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:36,009
Were these children offered to the gods?

9
00:00:36,009 --> 00:00:38,660
Or mourned, and given a burial of their own?

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:51,775
Carthage was a Phoenician city, linked to the great port of Tyre, some two thousand three hundred kilometres to the east.

11
00:00:51,775 --> 00:00:54,988
Tradition names its founder as a princess of Tyre:

12
00:00:54,988 --> 00:00:56,851
Elissa, better known as Dido.

13
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:01,825
It stood on the Gulf of Tunis, in what is now Tunisia.

14
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:14,561
From the sixth century BC it grew into a trading power whose reach spanned much of the Mediterranean, until Rome destroyed it in one hundred and forty-six BC.

15
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:20,225
Close to its ancient harbours lies an open-air sacred precinct.

16
00:01:20,225 --> 00:01:22,560
Archaeologists call it the tophet.

17
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:29,151
It is the same word the Hebrew Bible uses for a condemned place of child burning near Jerusalem.

18
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:32,675
The first excavators moved fast.

19
00:01:32,675 --> 00:01:43,345
In a single season in nineteen twenty-five, an expedition led by Francis Kelsey lifted out around a thousand urns, recording little about where each one lay.

20
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:57,268
It took until the nineteen-seventies, and an international campaign to save ancient Carthage, for a team led by the American archaeologist Lawrence Stager to dig the precinct layer by layer.

21
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:10,341
In a trench of roughly a hundred and fifty square metres they recovered hundreds of urns, buried over some six centuries, from around the eighth century BC until the city fell.

22
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:15,683
Inside were the cremated remains of very young infants.

23
00:02:15,683 --> 00:02:17,711
Some urns held only animals:

24
00:02:17,711 --> 00:02:20,029
mostly young lambs, a few birds.

25
00:02:20,029 --> 00:02:22,926
Some held an infant and a lamb together.

26
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:29,300
And in about three in ten of the urns studied, someone had placed small amulets.

27
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:33,301
They were only ever with human remains, and only after the fire.

28
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:38,756
Long before any excavation, Carthage already had a reputation.

29
00:02:38,756 --> 00:02:46,343
Writing in the first century BC, the Greek historian Diodorus Siculus described a siege in three hundred and ten BC.

30
00:02:46,343 --> 00:02:57,005
He claimed that the Carthaginians, fearing their gods had turned against them, sacrificed two hundred children of their noblest families to a god he called Cronus.

31
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:05,163
Later, the Greek writer Plutarch wrote of parents offering their own children, and of mothers forbidden to weep.

32
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:09,621
But these are accusations by outsiders:

33
00:03:09,621 --> 00:03:11,924
Greek authors, not Carthaginians.

34
00:03:11,924 --> 00:03:16,948
And Diodorus wrote more than two centuries after the siege he describes.

35
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:27,924
Jeffrey Schwartz's team points out that the sacrifice interpretation rests partly on accounts like these, which they call supposed eye-witness reports.

36
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:32,016
Hostile sources are not necessarily false.

37
00:03:32,016 --> 00:03:34,410
But on their own, they are not proof.

38
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:39,437
The Carthaginians themselves left something different:

39
00:03:39,437 --> 00:03:39,841
stone.

40
00:03:39,841 --> 00:03:42,332
Above many urns stand carved markers.

41
00:03:42,332 --> 00:03:49,201
Their inscriptions, in the Punic language, record a vow to the goddess Tanit and the god Ba'al Hammon,

42
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:51,740
and often a reason:

43
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:54,679
because the god heard his voice, and blessed him.

44
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,857
For Lawrence Stager, that wording was crucial.

45
00:03:58,857 --> 00:04:02,646
These were promised gifts, given after a prayer was answered.

46
00:04:02,646 --> 00:04:07,242
That, he argued, is hard to reconcile with an infant who had already died.

47
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:11,004
A few inscriptions use a word, molk.

48
00:04:11,004 --> 00:04:21,556
Following the scholar Otto Eissfeldt, many researchers, including Paolo Xella, read it as the name of a kind of sacrificial offering, not a god called Moloch.

49
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:25,121
Even the carvings divide experts.

50
00:04:25,121 --> 00:04:28,016
One stela shows a priest holding an infant.

51
00:04:28,016 --> 00:04:31,180
Stager saw a living child, about to be offered.

52
00:04:31,180 --> 00:04:34,613
Schwartz's team see an infant who has already died.

53
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,647
Which brings us to the bones themselves.

54
00:04:38,647 --> 00:04:42,815
Cremated infant bones are tiny, fragile, and distorted by fire.

55
00:04:42,815 --> 00:04:49,895
To estimate age at death, specialists measure fragments of the skull and hip, and examine developing teeth.

56
00:04:49,895 --> 00:04:54,130
The case for or against sacrifice depends largely on those ages.

57
00:04:55,500 --> 00:05:00,264
One clue is a faint line in the tooth enamel, called the neonatal line.

58
00:05:00,264 --> 00:05:04,827
It forms only if a baby survives at least a week or two after birth.

59
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:07,728
But fire shrinks bone.

60
00:05:07,728 --> 00:05:09,741
It may damage the enamel too.

61
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:15,234
How much you correct for that changes the answer.

62
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:24,929
Jeffrey Schwartz and his colleagues studied cremated remains from three hundred and forty-eight urns from the nineteen-seventies excavation.

63
00:05:24,929 --> 00:05:33,495
In their twenty seventeen summary, about a quarter of the individuals were prenatal, and many more died around birth, or within the first month.

64
00:05:33,495 --> 00:05:38,373
They found the neonatal line in twenty-four of the fifty individuals they sampled.

65
00:05:38,373 --> 00:05:42,775
To them, that pattern matches natural infant mortality in an ancient city.

66
00:05:42,775 --> 00:05:54,079
The tophet, they argue, was a burial place for those who died shortly before or after birth, whatever the cause, though they accept the Carthaginians may occasionally have sacrificed humans.

67
00:05:54,079 --> 00:05:57,588
Patricia Smith's team examined largely the same collection.

68
00:05:57,588 --> 00:06:07,047
Correcting for shrinkage, and arguing that cremation can erase the neonatal line, they concluded that most of the infants died at one to one and a half months:

69
00:06:07,047 --> 00:06:10,914
past birth, and in their view unlike an ordinary infant cemetery.

70
00:06:10,914 --> 00:06:13,115
To them, sacrifice was very probable.

71
00:06:14,500 --> 00:06:15,263
Same bones.

72
00:06:15,263 --> 00:06:16,165
Two verdicts.

73
00:06:16,165 --> 00:06:24,283
In exchanges running from twenty ten to twenty seventeen, each team argued in print that the other had made mistakes.

74
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:27,122
Carthage was not alone.

75
00:06:27,122 --> 00:06:34,666
Precincts like this appear across the central Mediterranean, in North Africa, Sardinia, Sicily, and perhaps Malta.

76
00:06:34,666 --> 00:06:39,629
Yet no convincing example has been found in the Phoenician homeland itself.

77
00:06:39,629 --> 00:06:44,592
Paolo Xella argues the custom still came from the east, probably from Tyre.

78
00:06:44,592 --> 00:06:46,313
The question remains open.

79
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:58,772
At Zita, in southern Tunisia, a tophet used after Carthage fell, researchers studying twelve cremated children found signs of hard lives and illness, and of careful burial.

80
00:06:58,772 --> 00:07:06,947
They found no concrete sign of sacrifice, but could not say whether the precinct was a cemetery, a place of sacrifice, or both.

81
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:16,847
And at Carthage, Tunisian-led excavations since twenty fourteen are dating the precinct's layers more precisely than ever before.

82
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:20,434
So what can the evidence establish?

83
00:07:20,434 --> 00:07:28,988
That for some six centuries, people here cremated infants and young animals, and buried them with care, under stones carved with vows.

84
00:07:28,988 --> 00:07:32,244
That their enemies accused them of child sacrifice.

85
00:07:32,244 --> 00:07:36,329
What it cannot yet establish is how each of these children died.

86
00:07:37,700 --> 00:07:47,583
In twenty twenty-four, one independent review of the newest research concluded that the interpretation of the tophet remains as uncertain as ever.

87
00:07:48,900 --> 00:07:57,140
A hundred years after that night in nineteen twenty-one, the urns have been excavated, the bones measured, the inscriptions read.

88
00:07:57,140 --> 00:08:00,461
And still, the evidence leaves two stories standing:

89
00:08:00,461 --> 00:08:05,060
children offered to the gods, or children mourned, and buried with care.

90
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:10,819
Perhaps the most honest thing archaeology can say is the hardest to hear.

91
00:08:10,819 --> 00:08:11,909
We don't know yet.

92
00:08:11,909 --> 00:08:15,904
But the answer may still be there, in the ground beneath Carthage.

